
  [1mThe fork: is there a server on the other side of this, or not?[0m Everything about a "real user id" and a "persistent
  record" depends on the answer, because a browser cannot mint a trustworthy identity or keep a record the user can't
  edit.

  - [1mA real backend exists (or will).[0m The server authenticates, and [3mit[0m issues the user id in the login response — [38;5;153mlogin[39m
    receives the id rather than taking it as a parameter. The record is written server-side, where it's actually a
    record. This is the only version where "who logged in" is trustworthy. Cost: the work is mostly not in this repo,
    and [38;5;153mlogin[39m has to become genuinely async against a real endpoint instead of a stub.
  - [1mNo backend, browser only.[0m The id has to be generated or stored client-side ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m), and so does the record.
    Be clear about what this buys: anyone can open devtools and change both, so it's usable as analytics or a
    convenience ("remember who was last here"), and worthless as an audit trail or anything security decisions rest on.
    Cheap now, and [3mnot[0m cheap to reverse — once other forms read a client-minted id, moving to server-issued ids means
    changing every consumer.
  - [1mBackend is planned but not yet built.[0m Design the client-side identity module against the interface the server will
    eventually satisfy, with the storage behind a seam you can swap. Costs a little indirection now, avoids the rewrite
    later.

  My recommendation depends on what you say next, but I'd push hard against the second option if the word "record" means
  anything compliance- or security-adjacent to you. A forgeable login log is worse than no log, because people trust
  it.
